# Fictional EP Tracklist

## 1. Gate Three Changes Without Reopening

**Story & Context:** The debut runs at 104 BPM. Luc begins with a rigid kick, clipped snare, and one-note analog bass pattern. Maarja holds a two-string hiiu kannel fifth across four bars. On the fifth bar she touches one string from behind without reversing the bow, turning the fifth into a narrower suspended interval while the machine pattern remains unchanged.

Liis holds a reed-organ octave throughout the first SHIFT. Amélie sustains the old upper pitch on bass oboe, causing a brief three-note friction field. Tõnu removes the programmed snare on every second pass and replaces it with one muted frame-drum rim stroke.

At the midpoint, Luc finally changes the bass to match Maarja's altered pitch, but she immediately releases back to OPEN. The machine arrives late to the new harmony and is instantly wrong again. The final section contains one continuous bow stroke lasting across several bars while every electronic layer mutes one by one.

**Visual & Lyrics Prompt:** Close view of a paper warehouse gate card marked 3 beside a hiiu kannel string diagram showing HOLD and SHIFT without a second attack mark, iron bale hook and analog sequencer lights in shallow focus. Sparse lyrics limited to “gate three,” “do not reopen,” “same bow,” and “wrong by the time you arrive.”

## 2. Bale Weight Remains on the Old Pitch

**Story & Context:** At 110 BPM, Luc programs the EP's most active minimal-synth bass line: root, fifth, octave, root. Maarja sustains one narrow bowed dyad and changes only the upper string through repeated back-finger TOUCH events. Each SHIFT creates a different relationship to the unchanged bass sequence.

Liis uses prepared reed-organ fifths with one damped reed that blooms late. Tõnu plays no continuous rhythm, only dry center hits where the electronic hats disappear. Amélie enters on a low fixed note after every fourth cycle, functioning like a calibration tone.

The track's central turn occurs when Luc mutes the bass but leaves the drum machine running. Without the electronic reference, Maarja's changing interval suddenly sounds less dissonant even though she has not altered her playing. When the bass returns, the old conflict reappears immediately.

**Visual & Lyrics Prompt:** Mechanical bale scale holding a flax bundle beside two parallel pitch lines, one fixed and one repeatedly stepping inward without new attack marks, faded cooperative-blue numbers on the wall. Lyrics should use plain language about weight, tolerance, old values, and a reading that stays valid after the material has changed.

## 3. The Bow Leaves After the Shift Whistle

**Story & Context:** The closer runs at 96 BPM with the sparsest electronics on the EP. Luc uses one kick every bar, one snare every two bars, and a mono bass note only at section boundaries. Maarja begins each phrase with a low open drone, then makes two or three internal pitch changes during the same bow stroke.

Liis sustains a minor reed-organ chord for sixteen bars without revoicing. Amélie holds one long bass-oboe note beneath the entire section. Tõnu uses a single frame-drum fingertip tap every four bars, so most perceived motion comes from the hiiu kannel's internal revoicing.

During the final minute, the old factory shift whistle is heard once as a dry field recording. No instrument responds immediately. Maarja completes one final SHIFT, releases to OPEN, and only then lifts the bow. The machines continue alone for four beats before Luc cuts power instead of fading.

**Visual & Lyrics Prompt:** Empty flax warehouse at winter dusk, hiiu kannel bow resting across two strings beside a faded shift-whistle notice and an inspection clock, pale window light crossing long plank floors. Sparse spoken lines limited to “shift whistle,” “finish the stroke,” “open string,” and “machines stay four beats.”

